.TH YESH 1 "2026-10-27" "yesh experimental" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yesh \- readable Linux automation with bounded process pipelines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yesh
.RB [ \-\-plan ]
.I SCRIPT.yesh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yesh
runs a small indentation-based automation language through its own
process runtime. It does not translate scripts to Bash.
A failing command stops the script by default.
.PP
This implementation is experimental. Read the script and its external
commands before granting access to privileged resources.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-plan
Validate commands and print expanded argument vectors and redirections
without running commands or opening redirected files.
Both branches of a command-based condition are shown.
.SH LANGUAGE
.SS Values and arguments
.nf
service = api
items = [one, 'two words']
echo $service "service"
printf '%s\en' $items
.fi
.PP
A variable expands only through an unquoted
.B $name
reference. A bare or quoted word is a literal argument, including empty strings.
Unknown or malformed variable references are errors, so adding an assignment
cannot change an existing command's meaning. The alternative
.B ${name}
form is not supported.
A scalar produces one argument; a list produces separate arguments.
Single quotes preserve raw text. Double quotes decode Go-style escape
sequences identically in assignments, lists and command arguments.
Values are never reinterpreted as pipeline operators, shell substitutions,
or glob patterns.
.PP
An external value may be read explicitly with
.B environment NAME
in an assignment:
.nf
expected_source = environment YESH_BACKUP_SOURCE
.fi
.PP
The name must be a valid environment variable name and its value must be
non-empty. Missing and empty values are errors. In plan mode the value is
masked; yesh does not print it as part of an expanded command.
.SS Blocks
.nf
if exists /etc/os-release:
    cat < /etc/os-release
else:
    echo "no release information"
for item in $items:
    echo $item
.fi
.PP
Blocks use spaces and indentation.
Loops must iterate over finite lists.
An ordinary nonzero command exit status makes a command condition false.
Timeouts, cancellation, missing commands and signal termination are errors.
The only file predicate form is "exists path", with one nonempty string.
Missing targets are false; permission failures and symlink loops are errors.
The old exists(path) spelling is rejected with a migration hint.
.PP
On Linux, an exclusive non-blocking advisory lock can scope a block:
.nf
with lock /var/lib/yesh/backup.lock:
    rsync -a source/ temporary/
    mv -- temporary current
.fi
.PP
The lock is released on success, error, cancellation, or script exit.
Plan mode displays the path without acquiring it. The parent directory and
permissions are the operator's responsibility; this is not a sandbox.
.PP
Use Bpublish source to destinationR for an atomic same-filesystem rename.
It never falls back to copying across filesystems; it provides namespace
atomicity, not power-loss durability.
.PP
Free space can be checked without parsing df output:
.nf
if free_space $target at_least 20GiB:
    rsync -a $source $target
.fi
.PP
The amount is a positive whole number with one of B, KiB, MiB, GiB or TiB.
The runtime reads filesystem statistics directly. Missing paths, unsupported
platforms, overflow and invalid units are errors.
.PP
The \fBmatches\fR condition compares one string with a \fBregex\fR value:
.nf
name = api-42
if $name matches regex '^api-[0-9]+$':
    echo "matched"
.fi
.PP
Regular expressions use Go's RE2 syntax. A regex value is for conditions and
cannot be passed as a command argument.
.SS Completion
.nf
if exists /etc/os-release:
    exit 0
exit 1
.fi
.PP
exit $code terminates the entire script, including nested blocks, with an
explicit decimal code from 0 to 255. It is a control statement, not an
external command or pipeline stage. In plan mode it is displayed without
ending plan traversal.
.SS Pipelines
.nf
cat < input.txt | sort | uniq > output.txt
printf '%s\en' next >> output.txt
.fi
.PP
A pipeline may contain at most 32 stages.
Input redirection is allowed once, on the first stage.
Output redirection is allowed once, on the last stage.
The first stage inherits standard input unless redirected.
Standard error is inherited independently of the pipeline.
.PP
Any failing stage stops the pipeline, including a writer killed by SIGPIPE
when a reader closes early.
Repeated or intermediate redirections, numbered descriptor redirections,
here-documents, background operators, shell command chaining and command
substitution are rejected.
.PP
On POSIX, redirects accept regular files and /dev/null.
Output symlinks and multiply linked regular files are rejected.
A regular file cannot be used as both input and output of one pipeline.
Appending is explicit with >>.
.SS Processes and signals
On POSIX each pipeline runs in a separate process group.
The default timeout is five minutes per command or pipeline, rather than
per entire script.
Cancellation or failure sends TERM to the group, followed by KILL after
a two-second grace period when necessary.
Direct children are waited for and descriptors are closed.
.PP
The CLI handles INT, TERM and HUP by cancelling the active command.
KILL cannot be handled. Processes that escape the group with a new session
require stronger isolation; full POSIX job control and PTY management
are not implemented. Windows process cleanup has different semantics.
.PP
The Linux read-only process snapshot uses an exact kernel name:
.nf
nginx = processes where name is "nginx"
show $nginx fields pid uid state rss cgroup
.fi
.PP
It prints selected fields from the fixed set pid, uid, state, rss, ppid,
pgid, sid, name and cgroup; RSS is in bytes. Without a fields clause it
prints the full set. The operation does not send signals or modify processes.
.PP
An RSS summary is available without parsing ps or awk:
.nf
summarize $nginx memory rss
.fi
.PP
It prints process count, total RSS, maximum RSS and integer average RSS in
binary units with exact byte counts in parentheses. RSS can count shared
pages more than once and is not the unique physical memory cost of a service.
.PP
An RSS budget can be checked for an existing process set:
.nf
if memory $nginx rss at_most 512MiB:
    echo "within budget"
else:
    exit 1
.fi
.PP
The condition sums RSS for that set. It does not rescan for a different set
and does not treat RSS as PSS.
Regex matching, arbitrary field selection and process control are not part
of this first snapshot interface.
.SS systemd service status and restart (Linux)
.nf
service status systemd-journald.service
service restart yesh-codex-fixture.service until active within 10s
service wait systemd-journald.service until active within 30s
journal systemd-journald.service since 15m lines 20
.fi
.PP
The restart line is a disposable-fixture example; inspect it with
--plan or run it only after creating that fixture. It is not a request to
restart a production service.
.PP
The status operation queries one exact unit name through a fixed
systemctl show property list. Restart runs systemctl restart and then
checks the exact Id and requested ActiveState within one finite timeout.
Wait performs an immediate read followed by bounded polling until the same
postcondition or deadline; it does not start or restart the unit.
It does not use systemctl --wait, does not add sudo, and does not imply
that a client timeout cancelled an operation inside systemd.
.PP
Unit names without a suffix, paths, globs, template units without an instance,
and shell operators are rejected. Aliases are rejected until canonical unit
identity can be reported explicitly.
An optional system or user manager profile may precede the unit. The omitted
profile means the system manager; the user profile adds --user to systemctl
or journalctl. The service and journal adapters are Linux-only. Empty journal
output is a successful empty result; diagnostics, invalid JSON, unavailable
bus, and permission failures are errors.
.SS Read-only storage (Linux)
.nf
backup = device by uuid "replace-with-real-uuid"
inspect $backup
require mounted /mnt/backup from $backup filesystem "ext4"
.fi
.PP
Use uuid or partuuid for an exact, unique identity. inspect prints the lookup
snapshot as JSON. require mounted repeats lookup and checks the exact directory,
device major:minor and filesystem; a directory merely contained in the root
mount fails. Symlink mountpoints and filesystem subtrees are rejected.
Whole-filesystem bind mounts cannot be distinguished. These are snapshots,
not protection against concurrent device/mount changes or a write permission test.
.PP
Queries require lsblk and findmnt (util-linux), have a ten-second execution limit plus a two-second cancellation grace period,
and accept at most 4 MiB per output stream. Missing/ambiguous identities and
query failures stop execution. Device values cannot become command arguments.
Plan mode defers all storage queries and path checks. Mount/unmount
are not implemented by this typed interface.
.SS fstab snapshots (Linux)
.nf
mounts = fstab "/etc/fstab"
inspect $mounts
.fi
.PP
Reads a regular-file snapshot through findmnt/libmount without device evaluation,
path canonicalization or mounting. Parser diagnostics fail the query even with
exit status zero. Empty files are valid. Limits: 4 MiB input, 64 KiB per line,
10,000 records; the private temporary snapshot is removed after the query.
.PP
Inspection prints ordered entries, dump/fsck numbers and duplicate-target
diagnostics. Swap entries sharing none are not duplicate targets. Option values
and non-local sources are hidden to avoid printing credentials; option order
and values are retained internally. Plan defers all reading.
.PP
This is configuration, not active mount or boot verification. nofail does not
prove storage readiness; noauto does not disable x-systemd.automount. systemd
handles passno differently from traditional fsck ordering. No fstab writing,
mount helpers, daemon-reload or systemd generator execution is performed.
.PP
To bind one configuration entry to its device and exact active mount:
.nf
mounts = fstab "/etc/fstab"
require mounted /mnt/backup from $mounts
.fi
.PP
Exactly one entry (including lexically equivalent paths) must match. Its
UUID/PARTUUID must resolve uniquely, and its explicit filesystem type and device
must match the kernel mount table. UUID/PARTUUID tags may have enclosing quotes.
Swap, path/LABEL sources, auto/type lists, bind/rbind, loop, move/remount and
btrfs subvolume options are unsupported and fail explicitly.
.PP
Kernel lookup disables canonicalization and precedes directory inspection;
an autofs-only target fails before traversal. nofail, noauto and automount
options never excuse a missing mount. Plan defers entry/device/mount checks.
The loaded fstab snapshot is used; later edits are not reread. This verifies
mountpoint, device and filesystem, not options, write access, free space or boot.
Concurrent mount/device changes still require an external boundary.
.SH SECURITY
Known shell interpreters are rejected after argument expansion on every stage.
Known code interpreters and execution wrappers are rejected in pipelines.
For example, curl piped to sh or data piped to xargs is rejected.
.PP
These checks prevent common dangerous forms; they are not a security
boundary for untrusted scripts.
External binaries, scripts with shebangs and tool-specific execution features
can execute arbitrary code and access resources allowed to the current user.
Use operating-system isolation for untrusted code.
.SH CRON
Use the absolute path to yesh and the absolute script filename in crontab.
Set an explicit PATH and use /dev/null as stdin for noninteractive jobs.
Use an external lock and an overall script timeout when appropriate.
Do not set crontab SHELL to yesh: cron expects a shell supporting -c.
.PP
The default timeout applies to each pipeline separately.
Package transactions may need a different policy and can be left incomplete
when interrupted. A timeout is not a transaction rollback.
.SH FILE PERMISSIONS
chmod and chown are external commands with their own link and privilege
semantics; yesh does not implement a typed permissions interface yet.
The sticky bit on a shared directory restricts removing and renaming
other users' entries. It does not protect writable file contents.
ACLs, immutable and append-only attributes, capabilities and SELinux or
AppArmor policy may restrict access independently of ordinary mode bits.
No such protection is disabled automatically.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
External commands inherit the caller's environment and working directory.
PATH controls executable lookup; implicit lookup from the current directory
is rejected by Go's default executable lookup policy.
There is no automatic sudo, user profile loading or environment sanitization.
Language variables are not automatically exported.
.SH EXIT STATUS
An explicit exit statement returns its requested code (0 through 255).
Otherwise the CLI returns:
.TP
.B 0
Successful execution or plan.
.TP
.B 1
Script read, parse or execution failure, including cancellation and timeout.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.
.SH FILES
.I SCRIPT.yesh
is read before execution.
Relative filenames are resolved against the inherited working directory,
not the script's directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
yesh /opt/ops/check.yesh
yesh --plan /opt/ops/check.yesh
man -l man/yesh.1
.fi
